# Summary
Bug introduced. The commit removed per-kind pending-command keys and replaced remove_pending_create_bucket_command with remove_pending_metadata_command_for_bucket in the existing-bucket CreateBucket path, allowing unrelated pending ACL/versioning commands to be deleted.
Pending metadata commands are now keyed only by (PG, bucket), so the pending entry may be a CreateBucket, PutBucketVersioning, or PutBucketAcl command. However, create_bucket_with_config_and_load_info still clears the pending entry as soon as the bucket exists on the primary. This happens before verifying the pending command kind and before the server-core owner check decides whether the CreateBucket caller actually owns the bucket. As a result, any authenticated caller who issues CreateBucket for an existing bucket during a partial metadata-command failure window can remove a pending ACL/versioning command. For the new ACL command path, this can leave replicas with divergent public_read/public_write state. A later retry with different ACL values can also become permanently conflicting because the partially-updated replica may already have the same execution generation with different ACL contents. If routing/failover later uses that stale replica, authorization decisions can incorrectly treat a private bucket as public-readable or public-writable.

# Reviewer note
This report was valid.

Fixed in commit `92523a82d60c2de49e173697e1eb1dcf93b3355c`.

The fix removes the unconditional pending-command clear from the existing-bucket CreateBucket path. Returning `Exists` is now a read of the bucket-primary state only; it does not mutate the per-bucket pending command stream. Successful command application paths still clear their own pending command after convergence, and finalized bucket delete remains the explicit bucket-incarnation cleanup point.

Regression coverage is in `storage::cluster::local::tests::existing_create_bucket_preserves_pending_acl_command_for_retry`. It reproduces a partial `PutBucketAcl(public_read=true)` apply, calls CreateBucket for the existing bucket with a different requested owner, verifies the pending ACL command is still present with the same command id/payload, and then retries the ACL command to convergence across all acting nodes.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm pending metadata commands are now keyed only by PG and bucket, not by command kind.
- [x] Confirm existing-bucket CreateBucket removes the bucket pending entry before checking the pending command payload kind.
- [x] Confirm a partial PutBucketAcl failure leaves a pending ACL command and divergent replica ACL state.
- [x] Confirm an existing-bucket CreateBucket call with a different owner can invoke the storage path that clears pending state before server-core ownership rejection.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce the unsafe state: pending ACL is present before CreateBucket, absent after CreateBucket, and a stale replica remains public-readable while the primary is private.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust regression PoC in the storage crate. Code review showed the pending metadata map is keyed only by `(PgId, BucketName)` (`crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:211-215`), and `remove_pending_metadata_command_for_bucket` removes whatever payload is stored there (`local.rs:287-295`). The existing-bucket CreateBucket fast path calls this unconditional remover before returning `Exists` (`crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:186-193`), while the ACL path depends on a failed partial `PutBucketAcl` remaining pending for retry/convergence (`request_ops.rs:725-779`). Server-core invokes storage CreateBucket before checking whether the existing bucket is owned by the caller (`crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket.rs:224-249`), so a non-owner request can reach the storage-side clear.

I added a narrow unit test PoC to `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs` that: creates a 3-node local cluster; creates a private bucket; injects a one-time failure during `PutBucketAcl(public_read=true)` application to leave a pending ACL command and one updated replica; then calls `create_bucket_with_config_and_load_info` for the already-existing bucket using an `attacker` owner. The test asserts the safe invariant that the pending ACL command should still exist. On the vulnerable commit, the test prints `after existing CreateBucket: pending_after_create=false, stale_replica_public_read=true, primary_public_read=false` and panics at the invariant, proving the unrelated pending ACL command was deleted and replica ACL state diverged.

Crash attempt: successful via targeted regression-test panic. Valgrind attempt: `valgrind` is not installed in the container. Debugger attempt: LLDB was run non-interactively with a breakpoint on `panic_fmt` and confirmed the panic stack reaches `storage::cluster::local::tests::poc_existing_create_clears_unrelated_pending_acl_command` at `local.rs:5395`.

